<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tikamgarh</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tikamgarh is a town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bundelkhand">Bundelkhand</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Formerly called Tehri, it became the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bundela">Bundela</a> state of Orchha in 1783 and remained so until the state joined India after independence. It is now the headquarters of Tikamgarh district.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tikamgarh, also spelled Tikamgadh, is a town and tehsil in Tikamgarh district in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a>. Its earlier name was Tehri, meaning a triangle, because it consisted of three hamlets forming a rough triangle. A locality called Purani Tehri, Old Tehri, still exists in the town. The town eventually took the name of the fort of Tikamgarh, and Tikam is a name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>. The district is known for the old fort of Kundar, Garh Kundar, which the Khangars built and which was the capital of Khangar rulers from 1180 to 1347.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-independence">Indian independence</a> in 1947 the area was part of the kingdom of Orchha, founded in 1501 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bundela">Bundela</a> chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudra-pratap-singh">Rudra Pratap Singh</a>, who became its first king and built the Fort of Orchha. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vir-singh-deo">Vir Singh Deo</a>, an ally of the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahangir">Jahangir</a> who reigned from 1605 to 1627, Orchha reached its height, and palaces such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahangir-mahal">Jahangir Mahal</a> remain from that time. In the early 17th century Jhujhar Singh rebelled against the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a>, whose armies devastated the state and occupied Orchha from 1635 to 1641. Orchha and Datia were the only Bundela states the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> did not subjugate in the 18th century.</p><p>In the later 18th century the capital moved from Orchha to Tehri, to the south, where the fort of Tikamgarh stood. Hamir Singh, who ruled from 1848 to 1874, was raised to the style of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> in 1865. His successor Pratap Singh, who reigned from 1874 and died in 1930, devoted himself to the development of the state and designed most of the engineering and irrigation works built in his reign. It was the oldest and highest in rank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bundela">Bundela</a> states. Pratap Singh's successor Vir Singh merged the state with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> on 1 January 1950. The district became part of Vindhya Pradesh, which merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a> in 1956.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Tikamgarh is the headquarters of its district and is governed as a municipality divided into 27 wards. At the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> its population was 79,106. It has become a centre for brass statues. The tradition of casting bronze statues was established in the 1950s by Bhainalal Soni, whose family made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> ornaments, and the President of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> awarded Harish Soni and Dhaniran Soni for their craftsmanship. A railway line from Lalitpur reached the town in 2013. Within about 10 kilometres are Kundeshwar, a temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, and Paporaji, a Jain site with many temples dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavira">Mahavira</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tikamgarh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1783 the capital moved to <b>Tikamgarh</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/orchha-state">Orchha State</a></li></ul></section>
